.TH VPNMAN 1 "October 2026" "vpnman 1.0.7" "User Commands"
.\" Written for man(7) / mdoc-free roff so it renders on every man implementation (man-db, mandoc, BSD).
.SH NAME
vpnman, vpnmand, vpnman\-gtk \- multi\-protocol VPN manager with a kill switch
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vpnman
[\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGUMENTS\fR]]
.br
.B vpnman
.B \-\-help
|
.B \-\-version
.br
.B vpnman
\fICOMMAND\fR
.B \-\-help
.br
.B vpnman\-gtk
[\fB\-\-background\fR]
.br
.B vpnmand
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B VPNMan
manages VPN connections from the terminal or from a GTK\ 4 / libadwaita desktop application.
It speaks OpenVPN, WireGuard and a long list of other protocols (see
.BR PROTOCOLS ),
keeps your traffic from leaking with a firewall\-based \fBkill switch\fR, reconnects and fails over
automatically, lets chosen applications and addresses bypass the VPN, connects on a schedule or on
untrusted networks, can put a VLESS/VMess/Trojan/Shadowsocks proxy (Xray) in front of or behind the VPN, and runs on systemd, runit, OpenRC, SysV init, OpenBSD and FreeBSD.
.PP
It consists of three programs:
.TP
.B vpnmand
The privileged background \fBdaemon\fR (run as root by the init system).
It owns the tunnels, the firewall rules, the DNS settings, the schedule and the network monitor.
Tunnels and firewall rules are only ever created or changed by the daemon, never by the front ends.
.TP
.B vpnman
The command line interface described in this page. Run without arguments in a terminal it shows an
interactive menu; otherwise it prints the connection status.
.TP
.B vpnman\-gtk
The graphical application (also started by \fBvpnman gui\fR or from the application menu).
.PP
The front ends talk to the daemon over a Unix socket (\fI/run/vpnman/vpnman.sock\fR). Who may use it is
described under
.BR "ACCESS CONTROL" .
Most commands therefore need no \fBsudo\fR; only installing, service control and cleanup do.
.SH QUICK START
.nf
sudo vpnman service enable        # once: start the background service now and at every boot
vpnman import work.ovpn           # add a profile (OpenVPN, WireGuard, ...)
vpnman connect Work               # connect and wait until the tunnel is up
vpnman lock on                    # engage the kill switch
vpnman leaktest                   # verify: tunnel, public IP, kill switch, DNS and IPv6
vpnman disconnect
.fi
.PP
Most installs (packages and \fBinstall.sh\fR) already enable the service, so the first line is usually not needed.
.PP
Anywhere a \fIPROFILE\fR is expected you may give the profile's id, its exact name, or any unique part of its name
(case\-insensitive). An ambiguous part of a name is refused with the list of matches.
.SH COMMANDS
Commands that talk to the daemon print \fBerror: ...\fR and exit with status 2 if it is not running
(start it with \fBsudo vpnman service enable\fR).
All commands accept \fB\-h\fR/\fB\-\-help\fR, which prints the options and examples of that command.
.SS Connecting
.TP
.B status \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR]
Show the state (disconnected, connecting, connected, reconnecting, disconnecting, error), the profile, the interface,
the public IP, uptime, traffic and rates, the kill switch, and the proxy when it is switched on. The exit status is 0 when connected and 3
otherwise, so \fBvpnman status >/dev/null\fR works as a test in scripts. \fB\-\-json\fR prints the raw status object.
.TP
.B connect \fR[\fIPROFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-fastest\fR] [\fB\-\-last\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-wait\fR] [\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR]
Connect and wait until the tunnel is up (default timeout 90 seconds), printing the progress. Without a
profile it reconnects the last one used. \fB\-\-fastest\fR measures every profile that is not blocked and uses
the one with the lowest latency. \fB\-\-no\-wait\fR returns at once. If a connection is already up it is replaced
by the new one (no double tunnels). If the server rejects the username or password, VPNMan stops retrying,
and in a terminal asks for new credentials, stores them and tries again. Alias:
.BR up .
.TP
.B disconnect
Take the VPN down. The kill switch is released too unless it was engaged by hand (\fBvpnman lock on\fR) or
\fBnetlock.persist\fR is set. Alias:
.BR down .
.TP
.B lock \fR[\fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR|\fBstatus\fR]
Engage, release or show the kill switch (see
.BR "KILL SWITCH" ).
A lock engaged with \fBon\fR stays until you run \fBoff\fR, even across disconnects.
.TP
.B ping \fR[\fIPROFILE\fR...]
Measure the round\-trip time to the servers (all profiles when none is named): a TCP connect for TCP servers,
ICMP ping for UDP ones. Unreachable servers are reported as such.
.SS Profiles
.TP
.B list \fR[\fB\-l\fR|\fB\-\-latency\fR] [\fB\-\-sort\fR \fIname\fR|\fIlatency\fR|\fIgroup\fR] [\fB\-\-names\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
List the profiles with protocol and server. A star marks favourites, a crossed circle marks blocked profiles,
and the group is shown in brackets. \fB\-\-latency\fR measures every server; \fB\-\-sort latency\fR implies it.
\fB\-\-names\fR prints one name per line (used by the shell completions). Alias:
.BR ls .
.TP
.B import \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIPATH\fR...
Import configuration files. The protocol is detected from the file name and contents; \fB\-\-protocol\fR forces
one. A directory is searched recursively (files ending in \fI.ovpn .conf .swanctl .vpnc .fortivpn .yml .yaml\fR)
and the name of the sub\-folder a file was found in becomes the profile's \fBgroup\fR. Certificates and keys a
config refers to by file name are copied along. Importing is safe to repeat: two profiles with the same name get
distinct names, and one failing file never stops the others (every failure is reported).
.IP
After the import, parts of an OpenVPN config that cannot work on this system are listed as \fBnote:\fR lines and
are ignored when connecting: Windows\-only options (\fBregister\-dns\fR, \fBblock\-outside\-dns\fR, \fBdhcp\-renew\fR,
\fBip\-win32\fR, \fBroute\-method\fR, ...), \fBup\fR/\fBdown\fR/\fBroute\-up\fR style hooks that point at missing
scripts or at DNS helpers such as \fBupdate\-resolv\-conf\fR (VPNMan sets the DNS servers itself), and a missing
CA certificate. DNS servers written into the config as \fBdhcp\-option DNS\fR are honoured.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-name " NAME"
Profile name (only with a single file; default: the file name without its extension).
.TP
.BI \-\-protocol " ID"
Skip detection. See \fBvpnman protocols\fR for the ids.
.TP
.BI \-\-user " USER"
Username to store.
.TP
.BI \-\-password " PASSWORD"
Password to store. It is visible to other users in the process list; prefer
.BR \-\-ask\-password .
.TP
.B \-\-ask\-password
Prompt for the password.
.TP
.BI \-\-stunnel " HOST[:PORT]"
Carry OpenVPN over TLS through stunnel (default port 443). See
.BR "SSL TUNNEL (STUNNEL)" .
.TP
.BI \-\-stunnel\-sni " NAME"
TLS server name to present.
.TP
.BI \-\-stunnel\-ca " FILE"
CA certificate with which to verify the stunnel server.
.TP
.BI \-\-stunnel\-verify " none|system|ca"
How to verify the stunnel server.
.RE
.TP
.B add \fIname\fR \fB\-\-protocol\fR \fIID\fR [\fB\-\-server\fR \fIHOST\fR] [\fB\-\-port\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-user\fR \fIUSER\fR] [\fB\-\-ask\-password\fR] [\fB\-o\fR|\fB\-\-option\fR \fIKEY=VALUE\fR]...
Create a profile without a config file, for protocols that need only a server and credentials (OpenConnect,
SSTP, PPTP, Tailscale, ...). \fB\-o\fR may be repeated for protocol options such as \fBexit_node=100.64.0.1\fR.
.TP
.B edit \fIPROFILE\fR \fIKEY=VALUE\fR...
Change fields: \fBname\fR, \fBusername\fR, \fBpassword\fR, \fBserver\fR, \fBport\fR, \fBdns\fR (comma separated
list that overrides the DNS servers for this profile), \fBnotes\fR, \fBgroup\fR, \fBfavorite\fR and \fBblacklisted\fR
(true/false), and the stunnel fields \fBstunnel=HOST:PORT\fR (or \fBoff\fR), \fBstunnel_sni\fR, \fBstunnel_ca=FILE\fR and
\fBstunnel_verify\fR.
.TP
.B remove \fIPROFILE\fR...
Delete profiles and their stored credentials. If one of them is connected, the VPN is disconnected first.
Alias:
.BR rm .
.TP
.B fav \fIPROFILE\fR...   unfav \fIPROFILE\fR...
Mark or unmark favourites. Favourites sort first and are the last resort of failover.
.TP
.B block \fIPROFILE\fR...   unblock \fIPROFILE\fR...
Blacklist a profile: \fB\-\-fastest\fR, the scheduler's \fBfastest\fR and failover never pick it (you can still
connect to it by name).
.TP
.B group \fR[\fIlist\fR]
.TQ
.B group edit \fIGROUP\fR \fIKEY=VALUE\fR... [\fB\-\-ask\-password\fR]
List the groups (a profile's group is set with \fBedit PROFILE group=NAME\fR, or comes from the folder it was
imported from), or change \fBevery server in a group at once\fR. Keys: \fBname\fR (renames the group),
\fBusername\fR, \fBpassword\fR (or \fB\-\-ask\-password\fR), \fBstunnel=HOST[:PORT]\fR (or \fBoff\fR),
\fBstunnel_sni\fR and \fBstunnel_verify\fR. Whatever is not named stays as it is. The SSL tunnel is applied to the
OpenVPN servers of the group only; the others are reported as skipped. \fIGROUP\fR may be a unique part of the name.
.TP
.B failover \fIPROFILE\fR [\fISERVER\fR...] [\fB\-\-clear\fR]
Show or set the servers tried, in order, when \fIPROFILE\fR keeps failing. See
.BR FAILOVER .
.SS Safety and privacy
.TP
.B bypass \fR[\fIlist\fR|\fIadd\fR|\fIremove\fR|\fIavailable\fR|\fIon\fR|\fIoff\fR|\fImode\fR] [\fIAPP\fR...]
Applications that skip the VPN. See
.BR "APP BYPASS" .
Alias:
.BR split .
.TP
.B routes \fR[\fIlist\fR|\fIadd\fR|\fIremove\fR] [\fIADDRESS\fR...]
Addresses, networks (CIDR) or domain names that always use your normal connection and are never blocked by the
kill switch. See
.BR "BYPASS ADDRESSES" .
.TP
.B dns \fR[\fIPRESET\fR | \fIIP\fR[,\fIIP\fR...] | \fBprovider\fR | \fBoff\fR]
Choose the DNS servers used while connected. Without an argument it shows the current choice and the presets
(Cloudflare, Cloudflare malware\-blocking, Google, Quad9, OpenDNS, AdGuard, Mullvad). \fBprovider\fR uses the
servers the VPN pushes; \fBoff\fR leaves your DNS settings untouched (this can leak queries). Takes effect
immediately if connected. See
.BR DNS .
.TP
.B leaktest \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR]
Test the live connection: that the tunnel is up, the public IP, that traffic outside the tunnel is really blocked
while the kill switch is engaged, that the DNS servers sit behind the tunnel and that IPv6 cannot bypass it.
When a proxy is switched on it is checked too: in \fBlocal\fR mode the public IP is also looked up through the local HTTP
proxy (it must differ from the direct one); in \fBsystem\fR mode the public IP should be the proxy server's address (a
different one is reported as a warning, because some servers send traffic out from another address); a proxy that is
switched on but not running fails. In system\-wide mode the kill switch probe carries Xray's socket mark so the redirect
does not mistake it for proxied traffic.
Prints a mark for each check (ok, warning, fail, info). Exit status 1 if a check fails. Connect first (or switch the
proxy on).
.TP
.B connections \fR[\fB\-l\fR|\fB\-\-listening\fR] [\fB\-\-local\fR] [\fB\-r\fR|\fB\-\-resolve\fR] [\fB\-w\fR|\fB\-\-watch\fR] [\fB\-\-csv\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
The network connections of this computer as the daemon sees them: the way (\fBin\fR, \fBout\fR, \fBlisten\fR), the
application and its pid, the protocol (TCP, UDP, with a 6 for IPv6), the local and remote address and port, and the TCP
state. A TCP connection is \fBin\fR when a local listener accepted it. Sockets that only wait for connections
(\fB\-\-listening\fR) and connections that never leave this computer (\fB\-\-local\fR) are hidden unless asked for.
\fB\-\-resolve\fR shows host names instead of addresses where reverse DNS knows them (the lookups are done in the
background by the daemon, so names appear a moment later; they are cached for ten minutes and cost DNS queries, which is
why it is off by default). \fB\-\-watch\fR refreshes every two seconds. \fB\-\-csv\fR prints CSV with a header line
(direction, application, pid, protocol, ip_version, local_address, local_port, remote_address, remote_port, remote_name,
state) for spreadsheets; values that look like formulas get a leading apostrophe. Linux reads \fI/proc/net\fR and finds the owner of every socket through
\fI/proc/PID/fd\fR; FreeBSD uses \fBsockstat\fR; other BSD systems use \fBnetstat\fR and show no application names.
The same table is on the Connection page of the application.
.TP
.B blocks \fR[\fIlist\fR|\fIadd\fR|\fIremove\fR|\fIenable\fR|\fIdisable\fR|\fIon\fR|\fIoff\fR] [\fIARG\fR...] [\fB\-\-proto\fR \fBany\fR|\fBtcp\fR|\fBudp\fR] [\fB\-\-note\fR \fITEXT\fR] [\fB\-\-for\fR \fIDURATION\fR] [\fB\-\-until\-reboot\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
Addresses, ports and programs that may not use the network. See
.BR "BLOCKED CONNECTIONS" .
\fBlist\fR (the default) shows the entries with their ids; \fBadd\fR \fIKIND\fR \fIVALUE\fR blocks one (\fIKIND\fR is
\fBaddress\fR, \fBendpoint\fR, \fBport\fR or \fBapp\fR; \fB\-\-proto\fR applies to endpoints and ports; \fB\-\-note\fR is a
reminder); \fBremove\fR \fIID\fR... unblocks; \fBenable\fR/\fBdisable\fR \fIID\fR... keep an entry but stop or resume
enforcing it; \fBon\fR/\fBoff\fR switch the whole list. A block can be temporary: \fB\-\-for\fR \fIDURATION\fR
(\fB30m\fR, \fB2h\fR, \fB1d\fR, \fB1h30m\fR, or a number of minutes; at most a year) ends it after that long, and
\fB\-\-until\-reboot\fR ends it when the computer restarts; together, whichever comes first. The list shows how long each
temporary entry has left.
.TP
.B proxy \fR[\fIlist\fR|\fIshow\fR|\fIlink\fR|\fIadd\fR|\fIsources\fR|\fIrefresh\fR|\fIremove\fR|\fIedit\fR|\fIuse\fR|\fIon\fR|\fIoff\fR|\fIorder\fR|\fImode\fR|\fIset\fR|\fIstatus\fR|\fIping\fR|\fIfailover\fR] [\fIARG\fR...]
Proxies served through Xray, alone or together with the VPN. See
.BR "PROXIES (XRAY)" .
.RS
.TP
.B proxy list \fR[\fB\-l\fR|\fB\-\-latency\fR] [\fB\-\-group\fR \fIGROUP\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
The proxies (or only those of \fIGROUP\fR); a dot marks the one in use.
.TP
.B proxy show \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR]
Everything known about one proxy: protocol, server, port, group, subscription, notes, favourite, in use.
.TP
.B proxy link \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-qr\fR]
Print the proxy's share link, to move it to a phone or another computer; \fB\-\-qr\fR also draws it as a QR code in the
terminal (dark on a light background). Anyone with the link can use the proxy. Proxies imported from an Xray JSON config
have no link.
.TP
.B proxy add \fISOURCE\fR... [\fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-group\fR \fIGROUP\fR]
Add proxies. A \fISOURCE\fR is a share link (\fBvless://\fR, \fBvmess://\fR, \fBtrojan://\fR, \fBss://\fR), a file that
holds links (or an Xray JSON config; \fB\-\fR reads standard input), or an \fBhttp(s)://\fR subscription address, which is downloaded as you (not by the
daemon) and remembered so that \fBrefresh\fR can update it. \fB\-\-group\fR names the group the proxies go into
(default: the subscription's host name). A proxy that is already present is not added twice; a line that cannot be
understood is reported and does not stop the others.
.TP
.B proxy sources \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR]
The subscription addresses, with how many proxies each holds.
.TP
.B proxy refresh \fR[\fIURL\fR...]
Download the subscriptions again (all of them, or the given ones): new servers appear, changed ones are updated, servers
that vanished from the list are removed. New servers join the group the subscription's proxies are already in. When the
proxy in use changed, Xray is restarted with its new settings.
.TP
.B proxy remove \fINAME\fR... | \fB\-\-group\fR \fIGROUP\fR | \fB\-\-all\fR
Delete the named proxies, every proxy of a group, or all of them. Removing the proxy in use switches the proxy off.
.TP
.B proxy edit \fINAME\fR \fIKEY=VALUE\fR...
Change \fBname\fR, \fBgroup\fR, \fBnotes\fR or \fBfavorite\fR.
.TP
.B proxy use \fINAME\fR   proxy use \-\-fastest \fR[\fB\-\-group\fR \fIGROUP\fR]
Choose the proxy to use, or the one whose server answers quickest: of \fIGROUP\fR, else of the favourites (when there
are any), else of all.
.TP
.B proxy failover \fR[\fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR]
Show or set \fBproxy.failover\fR: while the proxy runs (proxy only, VPN then proxy) VPNMan checks its server every 20
seconds; after three failed checks in a row, or when Xray stops three times within two minutes, it switches to the next
favourite proxy that answers (in name order, starting after the current one), else to the next proxy of the same group,
else to any other. Not used in the proxy\-then\-VPN path (the VPN's own reconnect handles that). \fBproxy status\fR shows
the last switch.
.TP
.B proxy on   proxy off
Switch the proxy on or off.
.TP
.B proxy order proxy_only\fR|\fBvpn_proxy\fR|\fBproxy_vpn
How the proxy and the VPN are combined (see PROXIES).
.TP
.B proxy mode local\fR|\fBsystem
A proxy for applications, or all TCP and DNS of this computer (Linux).
.TP
.B proxy set \fR[\fIKEY\fR \fIVALUE\fR]
\fBsocks_port\fR, \fBhttp_port\fR, \fBdns\fR, \fBudp\fR (\fBblock\fR or \fBdirect\fR), \fBorder\fR, \fBmode\fR. Without
arguments it prints their current values.
.TP
.B proxy status \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR]   \fBproxy ping \fR[\fINAME\fR...] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
Show whether it runs, with the addresses of the local proxies and the settings; or measure the connect time to the proxy
servers (16 at a time).
.RE
.TP
.B networks \fR[\fIshow\fR|\fItrust\fR|\fIuntrust\fR] [\fINAME\fR]
Show the current network and the trusted ones, or mark one as trusted (the current one when no \fINAME\fR is
given). See
.BR "TRUSTED NETWORKS" .
.TP
.B schedule \fR[\fIlist\fR|\fIadd\fR|\fIremove\fR|\fIenable\fR|\fIdisable\fR|\fIon\fR|\fIoff\fR] [\fIID\fR|\fINAME\fR] [options]
Connect at set times. See
.BR SCHEDULE .
.TP
.B autostart \fR[\fBoff\fR|\fBlast\fR|\fBfastest\fR|\fIPROFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-login\-app\fR \fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR]
Choose what the daemon connects to when the machine boots (setting \fBconnection.autoconnect\fR) and whether the
tray application starts at login for the current user (an XDG autostart entry
\fI~/.config/autostart/\:io.github.smiley_mcsmiles.VPNMan.desktop\fR). At boot the daemon waits up to
\fBconnection.autoconnect_wait\fR seconds for a network and keeps retrying until it connects.
.SS Maintenance
.TP
.B backup export\fR|\fBimport\fR \fIFILE\fR [\fB\-\-replace\fR] [\fB\-\-settings\fR] [\fB\-\-force\fR]
Write all profiles (with their passwords and private keys) and the settings to a single file, or restore them.
\fBexport\fR refuses to overwrite a file without \fB\-\-force\fR and writes it readable by you only. \fBimport\fR adds
the profiles that are missing and changes nothing else; \fB\-\-replace\fR first removes every profile on this machine
and restores the settings as well; \fB\-\-settings\fR restores the settings without \fB\-\-replace\fR. The backup is
an ordinary \fI.tar.gz\fR; it is \fBnot encrypted\fR, so keep it somewhere safe. Importing never trusts the archive: only the expected file names are accepted (no paths, links or devices) and
sizes are capped.
.TP
.B history \fR[\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-lines\fR \fILINES\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-clear\fR]
Recent connections: when, how long, traffic and why each one ended. The last 200 are kept in
\fI/etc/vpnman/history.json\fR.
.TP
.B logs \fR[\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-lines\fR \fILINES\fR] [\fB\-f\fR|\fB\-\-follow\fR]
The daemon's recent log (connections, output of the VPN programs, firewall changes, errors). \fB\-f\fR follows it.
The log is also written to \fI/var/log/vpnman.log\fR.
.TP
.B get \fR[\fIKEY\fR]   \fBset\fR \fIKEY\fR \fIVALUE\fR
Show or change settings. Keys are dotted (\fBnetlock.allow_lan\fR). A section or the whole tree is shown as JSON.
\fBset\fR takes effect immediately and is saved. Booleans accept true/false, on/off, yes/no, 1/0; lists are comma
separated. See
.BR SETTINGS .
.TP
.B update \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR]
Ask GitHub for the newest VPNMan release and compare it with the installed one. Runs as you (not through the
daemon); nothing is downloaded or installed. Exit status 10 means a newer version exists.
.TP
.B protocols \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR]
List every supported protocol, whether its programs are installed, and what to install if not; then the helper programs
\fBstunnel\fR (OpenVPN over TLS) and \fBxray\fR (proxies).
.TP
.B doctor \fR[\fB\-\-fix\fR]
Report distribution, init system, daemon, firewall, the launcher and icons, tray support and available protocols.
\fB\-\-fix\fR (root) copies the launcher and icons into \fI/usr/share\fR, rebuilds the icon caches and removes stale
bytecode; log out and in afterwards. This is the first thing to run if VPNMan is missing from the menu.
.TP
.B cleanup \fR[\fB\-\-force\fR]
(root) Remove everything VPNMan created in the system: kill\-switch rules, the app\-bypass table, routing rule and
table, and the cgroups. Refuses to run while the service is running unless \fB\-\-force\fR is given. Use it if a
crash left you offline. Uninstalling runs it for you.
.TP
.B service install\fR|\fBuninstall\fR|\fBenable\fR|\fBdisable\fR|\fBstart\fR|\fBstop\fR|\fBrestart\fR|\fBstatus\fR [\fB\-\-init\fR \fISYSTEM\fR]
(root) Install or control the background service with the init system of this machine (auto\-detected;
\fB\-\-init\fR forces \fIsystemd\fR, \fIrunit\fR, \fIopenrc\fR, \fIsysv\fR, \fIopenbsd\-rc\fR or \fIbsd\-rc\fR).
\fBenable\fR installs the definition if needed, enables it at boot and starts it; \fBdisable\fR does the reverse;
\fBinstall\fR/\fBuninstall\fR only add or remove the service definition.
.TP
.B daemon
(root) Run the daemon in the foreground. This is what the init system runs as \fBvpnmand\fR. For debugging, stop the
service first.
.TP
.B gui \fR[\fB\-\-background\fR]
Open the graphical application. \fB\-\-background\fR starts it minimised to the tray.
.TP
.B about
Credits, license and how to support the project.
.TP
.B shell \fR(alias \fBmenu\fR)
Open the interactive menu (also what plain \fBvpnman\fR does in a terminal).
.SH INTERACTIVE MENU
Plain \fBvpnman\fR in a terminal shows the status and a one\-key menu: \fBc\fR Connect, \fBf\fR Connect to fastest,
\fBd\fR Disconnect, \fBs\fR Servers / profiles (favourites, blocking, editing, removing), \fBl\fR Network lock,
\fBi\fR Import profile(s), \fBp\fR Preferences, \fBa\fR App bypass, \fBt\fR Schedule, \fBg\fR View log,
\fBx\fR System check and \fBq\fR Quit. It uses the same daemon calls as the commands above.
.SH GRAPHICAL APPLICATION
.B vpnman\-gtk
(or \fBvpnman gui\fR) needs PyGObject, GTK\ 4 and libadwaita 1.4 or newer; the traffic graph additionally needs
pycairo (without it a text line is shown instead). Its pages are:
.TP
.B Connection
Choose a server and connect or disconnect; status, public IP, uptime and a live traffic graph; a live
\fBConnections\fR table (way, application and pid, protocol, local and remote address and port, state, refreshed every two
seconds while the page is open, with a filter box and switches for listening sockets, local connections and \fBResolve names\fR; a
\fBPop out\fR button opens the same table in a window of its own with a \fBPause\fR button for reading it; right\-click a
row to \fBcopy\fR its remote or local address, the application or the whole row, to \fBforce\-close\fR the connection, to
\fBstop\fR or \fBforce\-kill\fR the application (only your own programs) and to \fBblock\fR the remote address, the address
and port, the remote or local port, or the application, each for good, for 15 minutes, an hour, a day or until the
computer restarts; \fBExport\fR copies the rows shown as CSV or saves them to a file; \fBBlocked connections\fR below the
table opens the window where the blocks are added (with how long they last), switched off and removed); recent connections; DNS servers; start at boot. The server drop\-down follows profile changes immediately.
.TP
.B Servers
Two sub\-tabs. \fBVPN Servers\fR: all profiles, with a search box on top, grouped by group name and sorted by favourites, name or latency (\fBTest
Latency\fR, or automatically every 5/15/30 minutes while the tab is open), and favourite and block buttons.
Each server's button follows the connection: \fBConnected\fR (greyed out, with a green dot) on the server in use,
\fBConnecting…\fR while it starts, and \fBConnect\fR on the others \- clicking another server switches to it (the old tunnel
is shut down completely first) without leaving the list. While a disconnect is under way the buttons, and the
Connection page's button, show \fBDisconnecting…\fR and cannot be clicked.
Select several servers with Ctrl+click, Shift+click or Ctrl+A and remove or \fBEdit\fR them together (Delete key,
or the selection bar). The \fBEdit\fR button next to a group's name renames the group and edits all its servers
at once; the same dialog, opened from the selection bar, edits just the selected servers. In it you can set the group,
the username and password, and enable, change or disable the SSL tunnel (stunnel) server for all of them; fields
left empty stay as they are. \fBAdd Profile\fR (and the \fB+\fR button in the header bar) imports files or adds a profile by
hand; editing a profile also sets its failover servers.
.RS
.PP
\fBProxy\fR: the proxies (add share links, load a file, or give a subscription address; \fBRefresh Subscriptions\fR
re\-downloads them; \fBUse Fastest\fR picks the quickest server), a check box to choose the one in use, latency,
\fBShare\fR (the share link as a QR code and as text, with a copy button), multi\-select with Ctrl+click, Shift+click or Ctrl+A to remove several proxies
at once (Delete key, or the selection bar), and above the list the controls: \fBUse the
proxy\fR (with its live state), \fBPath of your traffic\fR (proxy only, VPN then proxy, proxy then VPN), \fBMode\fR (local proxy or
system\-wide), the local addresses with copy buttons, \fBFail over to another proxy\fR, and the ports, DNS server and UDP
behaviour. A failover is announced with a notification.
.RE
.TP
.B Network Lock
The kill switch: engage it now, and its behaviour (see
.BR "KILL SWITCH" ).
.TP
.B Schedule
Times at which the VPN connects and disconnects.
.TP
.B Apps
Applications and addresses that skip the VPN, and the bypass mode.
.TP
.B Log
The daemon's log, live.
.PP
The main menu holds \fBPreferences\fR, \fBExport Backup\fR / \fBRestore Backup\fR, \fBTest connection\fR,
\fBCheck for Updates\fR, \fBAbout\fR and \fBQuit\fR. Preferences cover reliability (reconnect, retries, failover,
timeouts), extra OpenVPN arguments, DNS and the connection checks, trusted networks, and interface options:
notifications, running in the background (tray), automatic latency tests and the daily update check (off by
default).
.PP
A rejected login opens a prompt for the username and password and reconnects. Closing the window keeps the
application in the system tray when a tray is available (\fBrun in background\fR); \fBQuit\fR ends it. The tray menu
connects and disconnects, switches the kill switch, and, once a proxy is chosen, switches the proxy on or off. The GUI
holds no privileges; everything it does goes through the daemon.
.PP
All pop\-up windows and dialogs (the connection table window, blocked connections, profile and group editors, preferences, ...)
close with \fBEscape\fR or \fBCtrl+W\fR.
.SH KILL SWITCH
The network lock (kill switch) blocks all traffic that does not go through the VPN. While it is engaged only these
may send or receive packets:
.IP \(bu 2
the loopback interface;
.IP \(bu 2
the VPN tunnel interface(s) and the VPN server addresses (so the tunnel can be established and re\-established);
.IP \(bu 2
DHCP (\fBnetlock.allow_dhcp\fR) and the local network (\fBnetlock.allow_lan\fR: 10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16, 169.254/16,
multicast, and the IPv6 equivalents), ICMP echo if \fBnetlock.allow_ping\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
the addresses in \fBnetlock.whitelist_out\fR (outgoing) and \fBnetlock.whitelist_in\fR (incoming), and the addresses
added with \fBvpnman routes\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
the applications added with \fBvpnman bypass\fR (their traffic is recognised by a firewall mark).
.PP
Everything else, DNS included, is dropped. IPv6 is dropped entirely when \fBnetlock.block_ipv6\fR is on (the
default), which prevents IPv6 leaks on tunnels that carry only IPv4.
.PP
When it engages: \fBvpnman lock on\fR engages it by hand and it stays until \fBvpnman lock off\fR;
\fBnetlock.enabled\fR engages it automatically with every connection and releases it on disconnect;
\fBnetlock.persist\fR keeps it engaged after disconnecting and across reboots, so a machine is never exposed between
boot and the VPN coming up. A lock left behind by a crashed daemon is removed at the next start unless it was set to
persist or engaged by hand.
.PP
Back ends (\fBnetlock.backend\fR, default \fBauto\fR): \fBnftables\fR (table \fIinet vpnman\fR), \fBiptables\fR (chains
\fBVPNMAN_OUT\fR and \fBVPNMAN_IN\fR, plus ip6tables) and \fBpf\fR (OpenBSD, FreeBSD; loads a complete ruleset and
restores \fI/etc/pf.conf\fR, or disables pf if it was off, when released). \fBauto\fR prefers nftables, then pf, then
iptables. The nftables rules are loaded atomically. \fBvpnman cleanup\fR removes whatever any back end left.
.PP
Some protocols need extra rules; \fBvpnman protocols\fR and the profile editor show the caveat (PPTP is broken
cryptographically; policy\-based IPsec has no tunnel interface; Nebula and Tailscale talk to many peers directly and
those must be whitelisted).
.SH DNS
While connected VPNMan can replace the system's DNS servers so queries cannot leak (\fBdns.force\fR, default on). The
servers used, in order of priority, are \fBdns.servers\fR (if set), then the profile's own \fBdns\fR field, then the
ones the VPN server pushes. The previous \fI/etc/resolv.conf\fR is restored on disconnect, and also at daemon start if it
crashed. On systems with systemd\-resolved the servers are set on the tunnel link. Set \fBdns.force\fR to false (or
\fBvpnman dns off\fR) to leave DNS alone. Use \fBvpnman leaktest\fR to verify. The environment variable
\fBVPNMAN_RESOLV_CONF\fR overrides the file that is edited.
.PP
OpenVPN 2.7's built\-in DNS handling (\fB\-\-dns\-updown\fR) is switched off when supported, because VPNMan manages DNS
itself.
.SH APP BYPASS
Applications added with \fBvpnman bypass add\fR (or on the \fBApps\fR page) keep using your normal connection while
the VPN is up, and keep working even when the VPN drops under the kill switch. Applications are found from the
installed \fI.desktop\fR launchers (native, Flatpak and Snap; \fBvpnman bypass available\fR lists them) or may be
given by process name. VPNMan places the application's processes, and everything they start, in a control group
and routes that group's traffic outside the tunnel.
.PP
Modes (\fBvpnman bypass mode\fR, setting \fBsplit.mode\fR):
.TP
.B exclude
(default) the listed applications skip the VPN.
.TP
.B include
(experimental) \fBonly\fR the listed applications use the VPN; everything else uses the normal connection.
.PP
Requirements: Linux with \fBnft\fR, \fBip\fR (iproute2) and a \fBunified cgroup v2\fR hierarchy mounted at
\fI/sys/fs/cgroup\fR (hybrid cgroup layouts are not supported). It is not available on
the BSDs. Processes are moved within a couple of seconds of the tunnel coming up or of the application starting.
.PP
What it creates (all removed on disconnect, daemon stop, \fBvpnman cleanup\fR and uninstall): the nftables table
\fIinet vpnman_split\fR, the cgroups \fIvpnman\-bypass\fR and \fIvpnman\-tunnel\fR, firewall mark \fB0x5652\fR, an
\fBip rule\fR for that mark and routing table \fB5652\fR. VPNMan keeps its rule ahead of those added by
\fBwg\-quick\fR, so it works with WireGuard as well as OpenVPN. The bypass follows a new default gateway when the
network changes (for example Ethernet to Wi\-Fi).
.SH BYPASS ADDRESSES
\fBvpnman routes\fR keeps a list of IP addresses, networks (CIDR) and domain names that use the normal connection
instead of the tunnel. They are also allowed through the kill switch. Domain names are resolved when needed and
refreshed. Typical uses: a company intranet or printer, a banking site, a game server. The list is the setting
\fBroutes\fR.
.SH FAILOVER
When a connection fails \fBconnection.retry_max\fR times in a row (waiting \fBconnection.retry_delay\fR seconds
between attempts) and \fBconnection.failover\fR is on, VPNMan moves on to another server. The order is:
.IP 1. 3
the profile's own failover list (\fBvpnman failover PROFILE SERVER...\fR, or \fBFailover servers\fR in the profile
editor), in the order given;
.IP 2. 3
the other profiles in the same group, if \fBconnection.failover_group\fR is on;
.IP 3. 3
your favourites.
.PP
Blocked profiles and servers already tried in this round are skipped. A rejected username or password (\fBAUTH_FAILED\fR)
never causes retries or failover: it stops with a clear message, since another attempt would just repeat the
wrong credentials. With \fBconnection.reconnect\fR off a lost connection is not re\-established.
.SH SCHEDULE
\fBvpnman schedule add --start HH:MM [--end HH:MM] [--days SPEC] [--profile P] [--name N]\fR creates an entry. Times
are on a 24\-hour clock in the machine's local time. \fB\-\-days\fR accepts \fBall\fR, \fBweekdays\fR, \fBweekends\fR, a
list such as \fBmon,wed,fri\fR or a range such as \fBmon\-fri\fR. \fB\-\-profile\fR is a profile, \fBfastest\fR or \fBlast\fR
(the default). An entry with an end time is a window that connects at the start and disconnects at the end; a window
whose end is not later than its start runs past midnight and belongs to the day it started on. An entry without an end
only connects. The scheduler reacts to the start and end \fIedges\fR: a manual disconnect inside a window is respected
until the next edge. \fBvpnman schedule on|off\fR turns the whole scheduler on or off; entries can be enabled or
disabled individually. The scheduler runs inside the daemon, so it works with nobody logged in.
.SH TRUSTED NETWORKS
The daemon watches the network you are on. Wi\-Fi networks are identified by their name (SSID), wired ones by the
hardware address of the gateway. Mark networks you do not need the VPN on (home, office) with \fBvpnman networks trust\fR.
Then, with \fBnetwork.untrusted_action\fR set to \fBconnect\fR, VPNMan connects as soon as you join any network that is
not trusted (to \fBnetwork.profile\fR: \fBlast\fR, \fBfastest\fR or a profile), and with \fBnetwork.trusted_action\fR set to
\fBdisconnect\fR it disconnects again on a trusted one \(em only if it was the one that connected automatically.
.SH SSL TUNNEL (STUNNEL)
OpenVPN over TCP can be wrapped in TLS so that it looks like ordinary HTTPS (useful behind restrictive firewalls).
VPNMan runs \fBstunnel\fR in client mode on a local port and points OpenVPN at it. Enable it with
\fBvpnman import \-\-stunnel HOST[:PORT]\fR, \fBvpnman edit PROFILE stunnel=HOST:PORT\fR or in the profile editor.
\fBstunnel_verify\fR selects \fBnone\fR, \fBsystem\fR (the system CA bundle) or \fBca\fR (the certificate given with
\fBstunnel_ca\fR, stored in the profile); \fBstunnel_sni\fR sets the TLS server name. The \fBstunnel\fR program must be
installed.
.SH BLOCKED CONNECTIONS
The list of blocked things (setting \fBblocks\fR) is enforced by the nftables table \fIinet vpnman_block\fR, whose chains
run before the kill switch and the proxy rules. It is built when the daemon starts, rebuilt whenever the list changes and
removed when the daemon stops. An entry is one of:
.TP
.B address
An IP address or network (CIDR): nothing is sent to it and nothing is accepted from it.
.TP
.B endpoint
An address and a port with a protocol, for example \fB203.0.113.9:443\fR or \fB[2001:db8::1]:443\fR.
.TP
.B port
A port with a protocol: outgoing connections to it and incoming connections to a local service on it.
.TP
.B app
A program by name. Its processes (and everything they start) are kept in the cgroup \fIvpnman\-blocked\fR, whose outgoing
packets are dropped; running programs are found by a scan every two seconds, so nothing has to be started in a special way.
Needs cgroup v2 (the same layout as the app bypass); without it addresses and ports still work.
.PP
Blocking also \fBcloses the matching connections that are open right now\fR (with \fBss \-K\fR, which needs a kernel built
with CONFIG_INET_DIAG_DESTROY), because dropped packets alone would let an established TCP connection hang on for minutes.
Every entry can be switched off without deleting it, and can be temporary: it ends after a set time or when the
computer restarts (recognised by the kernel's boot id), checked every few seconds and when the daemon starts.
Blocking needs Linux with nftables; it is not available on the BSDs.
.PP
The GUI offers the same from the right\-click menu of the connection table and from the \fBBlocked connections\fR window.
Blocks apply to everybody on the computer. \fBvpnman cleanup\fR and uninstalling remove the table and the cgroup.
.SH PROXIES (XRAY)
VPNMan can use VLESS, VMess, Trojan and Shadowsocks servers (including Reality, WebSocket, gRPC, HTTPUpgrade, XHTTP,
mKCP and HTTP/2 transports) through \fBXray\fR (https://github.com/XTLS/Xray\-core), a single self\-contained program
that is not part of VPNMan: install the \fBxray\fR package of your distribution if it has one, or the release binary for
your system (Xray publishes builds for Linux, FreeBSD, OpenBSD and others) and put it where \fBPATH\fR finds it
(\fI/usr/local/bin\fR is searched too). \fBvpnman doctor\fR reports whether it is found. \fBsudo ./install.sh \-\-xray\-only\fR (from the source directory) downloads and installs it for you.
.PP
Proxies are stored like VPN profiles, root\-only in \fI/etc/vpnman/proxies/\fR, and are managed in the \fBProxy\fR
sub\-tab of the Servers page or with \fBvpnman proxy\fR. Share links and subscriptions are converted to Xray's
configuration when they are imported; the server address is resolved by the daemon, so Xray never needs DNS (which matters
while the kill switch is up), and clients never see the credentials inside a link.
.SS Order: how the proxy and the VPN are combined (proxy.order)
The names describe the path your traffic takes:
.TP
.B proxy_only
You \(-> proxy \(-> internet. Xray runs whenever the proxy is switched on, with or without a VPN. Websites see the
proxy server's address.
.TP
.B vpn_proxy
You \(-> VPN \(-> proxy \(-> internet. Xray runs \fIinside\fR the tunnel: it is started once the VPN is connected (so its
own connection to the proxy server goes through the tunnel) and stopped when the VPN goes down or reconnects. Websites see
the proxy server's address; the proxy server only sees the VPN's address. The kill switch needs no exception for the
proxy server.
.TP
.B proxy_vpn
You \(-> proxy \(-> VPN \(-> internet. The VPN's own packets travel through the proxy: VPNMan starts Xray before the VPN,
which then connects to a local forwarder (\fI127.0.0.1\fR) instead of the VPN server, exactly like the SSL tunnel (stunnel)
does. Your ISP only sees the proxy; websites see the VPN's address. Works with \fBOpenVPN\fR (UDP and TCP) and
\fBWireGuard\fR/AmneziaWG profiles with a single peer; other protocols are refused with a message, and so is a profile
that also uses the SSL tunnel. The kill switch lets only the proxy server through, and a host route keeps the proxy server's
traffic outside the VPN (it follows the default gateway when you change from Wi\-Fi to Ethernet). UDP VPNs need a proxy
server that carries UDP (VLESS, VMess, Trojan and Shadowsocks do); a TCP profile is the more dependable choice.
.SS Mode (proxy.mode)
.TP
.B local
Xray offers a SOCKS5 proxy (\fBproxy.socks_port\fR, default 10808) and an HTTP proxy (\fBproxy.http_port\fR, default 10809),
both on \fI127.0.0.1\fR only, never on the network. Point a browser or program at them, or copy the address from the
Proxy tab. Nothing else changes on the system.
.TP
.B system
(Linux with nftables.) In addition, every TCP connection and every DNS query of this computer is redirected to Xray with
the nftables table \fIinet vpnman_proxy\fR, so programs need no settings. Xray's own connections carry the socket mark
\fB0x5658\fR and are left alone. Not redirected: loopback, the local network (if \fBnetlock.allow_lan\fR), the VPN server
(in \fBvpn_proxy\fR), the bypass addresses (\fBvpnman routes\fR) and the applications of the app bypass. DNS queries are answered by
\fBproxy.dns\fR (default 1.1.1.1) \fIthrough the proxy\fR. \fBIPv6 is blocked\fR in this mode, and so is UDP other than DNS
(QUIC, NTP, voice and game traffic) unless \fBproxy.udp\fR is \fBdirect\fR, in which case that UDP leaves around the
proxy. System\-wide mode is not available on the BSDs; \fBlocal\fR works everywhere Xray does.
.SS Behaviour to know
.IP \(bu 2
A proxy switched on without a kill switch is not a guarantee: if Xray stops, traffic may go out directly. Combine it with
the kill switch (\fBvpnman lock on\fR): in \fBproxy_only\fR the proxy server is let through automatically.
.IP \(bu 2
Xray is restarted if it dies, and a stale Xray or stale firewall rules left by a crash are removed when the daemon starts.
\fBvpnman cleanup\fR removes the proxy's firewall table as well.
.IP \(bu 2
Subscriptions are downloaded by the program you run (the command line or the GUI), as you; the downloaded text is given to
the daemon. Nothing is fetched automatically.
.IP \(bu 2
Shadowsocks plugins (obfs, v2ray\-plugin) are not supported; use the matching Xray transport instead.
.IP \(bu 2
Backups (\fBvpnman backup\fR) include the proxies.
.SH PROTOCOLS
The protocol of a profile decides which program VPNMan runs. \fBvpnman protocols\fR shows which are installed.
.TP
\fBopenvpn\fR
OpenVPN (.ovpn) \(em openvpn
.TP
\fBwireguard\fR
WireGuard (wg\-quick .conf) \(em wg, wg\-quick
.TP
\fBamneziawg\fR
AmneziaWG (obfuscated WireGuard) \(em awg, awg\-quick
.TP
\fBopenconnect\fR
AnyConnect, GlobalProtect, Pulse, F5, ... \(em openconnect
.TP
\fBopenfortivpn\fR
Fortinet SSL VPN \(em openfortivpn
.TP
\fBikev2\fR
IKEv2/IPsec (swanctl.conf) \(em swanctl (strongSwan)
.TP
\fBsstp\fR
Microsoft SSTP \(em sstpc, pppd
.TP
\fBpptp\fR
PPTP (legacy, insecure) \(em pppd, pptp
.TP
\fBvpnc\fR
Cisco IPsec \(em vpnc
.TP
\fBtailscale\fR
Tailscale (optional exit node) \(em tailscale
.TP
\fBnetbird\fR
NetBird \(em netbird
.TP
\fBzerotier\fR
ZeroTier (network id) \(em zerotier\-cli
.TP
\fBnebula\fR
Slack Nebula \(em nebula
.TP
\fBnetworkmanager\fR
a VPN defined in NetworkManager \(em nmcli
.TP
\fBcustom\fR
your own connect/disconnect commands \(em (none)
.PP
For \fBcustom\fR profiles the commands may use the placeholders \fB{config}\fR, \fB{dir}\fR and \fB{iface}\fR.
Optional helpers: \fBstunnel\fR (SSL tunnel), \fBnft\fR/\fBiptables\fR/\fBpfctl\fR (kill switch), \fBip\fR and
cgroup v2 (app bypass), \fBresolvconf\fR or systemd\-resolved (DNS), \fBloginctl\fR (access control).
.SH SETTINGS
The settings live in \fI/etc/vpnman/settings.json\fR (root\-owned, mode 600) and are changed with \fBvpnman set\fR,
\fBvpnman dns\fR, \fBvpnman autostart\fR and the GUI. Sending \fBSIGHUP\fR to \fBvpnmand\fR re\-reads the file after a
manual edit. Unknown keys and values of the wrong type are ignored.
.PP
.B netlock
.TP
.B netlock.enabled \fR(false)
Engage the kill switch automatically on every connection.
.TP
.B netlock.persist \fR(false)
Keep it engaged after disconnecting and across reboots.
.TP
.B netlock.backend \fR(auto)
\fBauto\fR, \fBnftables\fR, \fBiptables\fR or \fBpf\fR.
.TP
.B netlock.allow_lan \fR(true)
Allow private, link\-local and multicast networks.
.TP
.B netlock.allow_dhcp \fR(true)
Allow DHCP so a lease can be renewed.
.TP
.B netlock.allow_ping \fR(false)
Allow ICMP echo.
.TP
.B netlock.block_ipv6 \fR(true)
Drop all IPv6.
.TP
.B netlock.whitelist_in\fR, \fBnetlock.whitelist_out \fR(empty)
IPs or CIDR networks allowed to reach this machine / that it may reach outside the tunnel.
.PP
.B dns
.TP
.B dns.force \fR(true)
Replace the DNS servers while connected.
.TP
.B dns.servers \fR(empty)
Custom DNS servers; they override the pushed ones.
.PP
.B connection
.TP
.B connection.autoconnect \fR(off)
At boot: \fBoff\fR, \fBlast\fR, \fBfastest\fR or a profile id/name.
.TP
.B connection.autoconnect_wait \fR(120)
Seconds to wait for a network at boot before giving up waiting (it then keeps retrying).
.TP
.B connection.reconnect \fR(true)
Re\-establish a lost connection.
.TP
.B connection.retry_max \fR(3), \fBconnection.retry_delay \fR(5)
Attempts before failing over or giving up, and seconds between attempts.
.TP
.B connection.failover \fR(true), \fBconnection.failover_group \fR(true)
Fail over to another server, and prefer servers of the same group.
.TP
.B connection.timeout \fR(60)
Seconds to wait for a tunnel to come up.
.TP
.B connection.openvpn_args \fR(empty)
Extra raw arguments appended to the OpenVPN command line.
.PP
.B access
.TP
.B access.mode \fR(session)
\fBsession\fR or \fBgroup\fR (see ACCESS CONTROL).
.TP
.B access.groups \fR(vpnman, wheel, sudo)
Groups whose members may use the daemon.
.PP
.B network
.TP
.B network.trusted \fR(empty)
Trusted networks (set with \fBvpnman networks trust\fR).
.TP
.B network.untrusted_action \fR(off), \fBnetwork.trusted_action \fR(off)
\fBconnect\fR / \fBdisconnect\fR, see TRUSTED NETWORKS.
.TP
.B network.profile \fR(last)
What to connect to on an untrusted network.
.PP
.B schedule
.TP
.B schedule.enabled \fR(true), \fBschedule.entries\fR
The scheduler switch and its entries (managed with \fBvpnman schedule\fR).
.PP
.B split
.TP
.B split.enabled \fR(true), \fBsplit.mode \fR(exclude), \fBsplit.apps\fR
App bypass switch, mode and the applications (managed with \fBvpnman bypass\fR).
.PP
.B routes
.TP
.B routes
Addresses that skip the VPN (managed with \fBvpnman routes\fR).
.PP
.B blocks
.TP
.B blocks.enabled \fR(true)
Enforce the list of blocked connections.
.TP
.B blocks.entries \fR(empty)
The list itself (managed with \fBvpnman blocks\fR and the Blocked connections window): each entry has an id, a kind,
a value, a protocol, a note, an enabled flag, the time it was added, and for temporary entries \fBexpires\fR (a Unix
time; 0 means never) and \fBboot\fR (the boot id it was made in; empty means it survives restarts).
.PP
.B proxy
.TP
.B proxy.enabled \fR(false)
Use the selected proxy.
.TP
.B proxy.selected \fR(empty)
Id of the proxy in use (set with \fBvpnman proxy use\fR).
.TP
.B proxy.order \fR(proxy_only)
\fBproxy_only\fR, \fBvpn_proxy\fR or \fBproxy_vpn\fR (see PROXIES).
.TP
.B proxy.mode \fR(local)
\fBlocal\fR or \fBsystem\fR.
.TP
.B proxy.socks_port \fR(10808), \fBproxy.http_port \fR(10809)
Ports of the local SOCKS5 and HTTP proxies (1024\-65535, different from each other).
.TP
.B proxy.dns \fR(1.1.1.1)
System\-wide mode: the resolver that answers redirected DNS queries (asked through the proxy).
.TP
.B proxy.udp \fR(block)
System\-wide mode: \fBblock\fR drops UDP other than DNS; \fBdirect\fR lets it bypass the proxy.
.TP
.B proxy.failover \fR(false)
Switch to another proxy when the server stops answering (see \fBvpnman proxy failover\fR).
.PP
.B events
.TP
.B events.pre_connect\fR, \fBevents.connected\fR, \fBevents.disconnected \fR(empty)
Commands run through \fB/bin/sh \-c\fR as root (60 second limit) before connecting, once the tunnel is up and after it
goes down. See
.BR "EVENT HOOKS" .
.PP
.B checks
.TP
.B checks.tunnel \fR(true)
Look up the public IP after connecting.
.TP
.B checks.url \fR(https://api.ipify.org?format=json)
The address asked for the public IP (the lookup goes through the tunnel).
.PP
.B ui
.TP
.B ui.notifications \fR(true)
Desktop notifications.
.SH EVENT HOOKS
The commands in \fBevents.pre_connect\fR, \fBevents.connected\fR and \fBevents.disconnected\fR run as root with these
environment variables set: \fBVPNMAN_EVENT\fR (the hook name), \fBVPNMAN_PROFILE\fR (profile name),
\fBVPNMAN_PROTOCOL\fR and \fBVPNMAN_IFACE\fR (the tunnel interface, empty before it exists). Their output is written to the
log. A hook that fails or takes longer than 60 seconds is logged and otherwise ignored. Example:
.PP
.nf
vpnman set events.connected 'logger "VPN up on $VPNMAN_IFACE via $VPNMAN_PROFILE"'
.fi
.SH ACCESS CONTROL
The daemon's socket accepts connections from every local user, and each connection is checked (on Linux by the peer
credentials the kernel supplies). Allowed: \fBroot\fR; members of the groups in \fBaccess.groups\fR (default
\fBvpnman\fR, \fBwheel\fR, \fBsudo\fR); and, in the default \fBaccess.mode session\fR, any regular user with an active
\fIlocal\fR login session (the trust model of desktop network managers, so no group membership or re\-login is
needed). On systems without a session manager any regular local user is allowed. Remote (ssh) sessions are not
trusted by \fBsession\fR mode. With \fBaccess.mode group\fR only root and group members may connect. Decisions are cached
for a few seconds. The installer adds the user who ran \fBsudo\fR to the \fBvpnman\fR group.
.PP
Stored passwords never leave the daemon: clients only see whether a password exists.
.SH INSTALLATION
Install a package (\fB.deb\fR, \fB.rpm\fR, Arch \fB.pkg.tar.xz\fR; recipes for Void, Alpine and OpenBSD are generated
by \fBpackage.sh\fR), or from the source tarball:
.PP
.nf
tar xf vpnman-VERSION.tar.gz && cd vpnman-VERSION
sudo ./install.sh                       # to /usr/local, service enabled for this machine
sudo ./install.sh \-\-prefix /usr         # another prefix (recommended on desktops)
sudo ./install.sh \-\-install\-deps        # also install dependencies with the native package manager (and Xray)
sudo ./install.sh \-\-xray\-only           # only download and install Xray, the engine of "vpnman proxy"
\&./install.sh \-\-check                   # only report what is missing
.fi
.PP
\fBinstall.sh\fR options: \fB\-\-prefix DIR\fR; \fB\-\-init SYSTEM\fR (\fIauto\fR, \fIsystemd\fR, \fIsysv\fR, \fIopenrc\fR,
\fIrunit\fR, \fIopenbsd\fR, \fIfreebsd\fR, \fIall\-linux\fR, \fInone\fR); \fB\-\-no\-post\fR (do not create the group, touch
caches or enable the service); \fB\-\-no\-system\-links\fR; \fB\-\-install\-deps\fR; \fB\-\-install\-xray\fR; \fB\-\-xray\-only\fR; \fB\-\-check\fR; \fB\-\-uninstall\fR and
\fB\-\-purge\fR. \fB\-\-install\-xray\fR downloads the official Xray release for this machine (Linux, FreeBSD or OpenBSD; it needs curl or
wget), verifies it against the checksum published next to it (a mismatch aborts, a missing checksum file only warns) and installs the
single program as \fIPREFIX\fR/bin/xray, replacing an older copy. Xray needs no service of its own, so this works on any init
system. \fB\-\-xray\-only\fR does just that (as a normal user if \fIPREFIX\fR/bin is writable); \fB\-\-install\-deps\fR does it
when Xray is missing. \fB\-\-uninstall\fR removes an Xray that this installer put there and leaves any other copy alone.
With \fBDESTDIR\fR set it stages files for packaging. It supports apt, dnf, pacman, xbps, apk, zypper,
pkg_add and pkg for dependencies, installs wrapper scripts that choose a Python interpreter that really has
PyGObject (so desktop launchers work even when another Python comes first in \fBPATH\fR), copies the launcher and
icons into \fI/usr/share\fR when installing elsewhere (some desktops ignore \fI/usr/local/share\fR), restores SELinux
contexts, creates the \fBvpnman\fR group, and waits for the service to answer, printing diagnostics if it does not.
.PP
Requirements: Python 3.9 or newer; for the GUI PyGObject, GTK\ 4 and libadwaita 1.4+ (plus pycairo for the traffic
graph, and the XApp/GTK\ 3 typelibs for the tray on Cinnamon); the program of each protocol you use; and one of
\fBnft\fR, \fBiptables\fR or \fBpfctl\fR for the kill switch.
.SS Where things are installed
With the default prefix \fI/usr/local\fR (packages use \fI/usr\fR):
.TP
\fIPREFIX\fR/bin/vpnman, vpnmand, vpnman\-gtk
the programs (small wrapper scripts)
.TP
\fIPREFIX\fR/lib/vpnman/vpnman/
the Python package
.TP
\fIPREFIX\fR/share/vpnman/
init templates and a private icon copy
.TP
\fIPREFIX\fR/share/applications/
io.github.smiley_mcsmiles.VPNMan.desktop
.TP
\fIPREFIX\fR/share/icons/hicolor/, share/pixmaps/
icons
.TP
\fIPREFIX\fR/share/metainfo/
AppStream metadata (also the release notes)
.TP
\fIPREFIX\fR/share/man/man1/vpnman.1
this manual page
.TP
\fIPREFIX\fR/share/bash\-completion/completions/vpnman
bash completion
.TP
\fIPREFIX\fR/share/zsh/site\-functions/_vpnman
zsh completion
.TP
\fIPREFIX\fR/share/fish/vendor_completions.d/vpnman.fish
fish completion
.TP
/usr/lib/systemd/system/vpnmand.service
systemd unit (/etc/systemd/system for other prefixes)
.TP
/etc/sv/vpnmand/ (or /etc/runit/sv/vpnmand/)
runit service, linked into /var/service or /etc/service
.TP
/etc/init.d/vpnmand
OpenRC and SysV init script
.TP
/etc/rc.d/vpnmand, /usr/local/etc/rc.d/vpnmand
OpenBSD and FreeBSD rc scripts
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/vpnman/
Configuration directory, root only (mode 700). Override with \fBVPNMAN_CONFIG_DIR\fR.
.TP
.I /etc/vpnman/profiles/ID/
One directory per profile: \fIprofile.json\fR (name, protocol, server, credentials, group, failover list, options) and the
config files, certificates and keys it needs. Mode 700/600 because it holds credentials.
.TP
.I /etc/vpnman/proxies/ID/
One directory per proxy (same layout as the profiles; \fIprofile.json\fR holds the share link and the Xray outbound with
its credentials), root only.
.TP
.I /etc/vpnman/settings.json
The settings (see SETTINGS).
.TP
.I /etc/vpnman/state.json
The last connected profile and whether the kill switch was engaged by hand.
.TP
.I /etc/vpnman/history.json
Connection history (last 200).
.TP
.I /run/vpnman/vpnman.sock
The daemon socket (\fI/var/run/vpnman/\fR on the BSDs). Override with \fBVPNMAN_SOCKET\fR / \fBVPNMAN_RUN_DIR\fR.
.TP
.I /run/vpnman/vpnmand.pid
The daemon's process id.
.TP
.I /run/vpnman/proxy/
Xray's runtime configuration (\fIconfig.json\fR, mode 600) and \fIxray.pid\fR while it runs.
.TP
.I /run/vpnman/conn/ID/
Runtime files of a live connection (the config copy given to the VPN program, a credentials file, the stunnel
configuration). Removed on disconnect and on reboot.
.TP
.I /var/log/vpnman.log
Daemon log (override with \fBVPNMAN_LOG_FILE\fR). Under runit also \fI/var/log/vpnmand/current\fR; under OpenRC and SysV
\fI/var/log/vpnmand.out\fR; under systemd \fBjournalctl \-u vpnmand\fR.
.TP
.I ~/.config/vpnman/gui.json
Per\-user GUI preferences (run in background, server sort, automatic latency test, update check).
.TP
.I ~/.config/autostart/io.github.smiley_mcsmiles.VPNMan.desktop
Created by \fBvpnman autostart \-\-login\-app on\fR.
.PP
Firewall objects created while running (all removed again by \fBvpnman cleanup\fR): nftables tables \fIinet vpnman\fR,
\fIinet vpnman_split\fR, \fIinet vpnman_proxy\fR (system\-wide proxy mode) and \fIinet vpnman_block\fR (blocked connections); iptables chains \fBVPNMAN_OUT\fR and \fBVPNMAN_IN\fR; cgroups \fI/sys/fs/cgroup/vpnman\-bypass\fR,
\fI/sys/fs/cgroup/vpnman\-tunnel\fR and \fI/sys/fs/cgroup/vpnman\-blocked\fR; an \fBip rule\fR with fwmark 0x5652 and routing table 5652.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VPNMAN_CONFIG_DIR, VPNMAN_RUN_DIR, VPNMAN_SOCKET, VPNMAN_LOG_FILE
Relocate the configuration directory, runtime directory, socket and log file (used by tests and unprivileged trials).
.TP
.B VPNMAN_ALLOW_UNPRIVILEGED
Let \fBvpnmand\fR start without root (development only; tunnels and firewall changes will fail).
.TP
.B VPNMAN_INIT
Force the init system detected by \fBvpnman service\fR.
.TP
.B VPNMAN_PYTHON
Force the Python interpreter used by the installed wrapper scripts.
.TP
.B VPNMAN_DATA_DIR
Where the wrappers keep the init templates and private icons (set by the wrappers).
.TP
.B VPNMAN_RESOLV_CONF
The resolver file VPNMan edits (default \fI/etc/resolv.conf\fR).
.TP
.B VPNMAN_TRAY
Set to \fBsni\fR to use the StatusNotifier tray even on Cinnamon.
.TP
.B VPNMAN_NO_REEXEC
Set to \fB1\fR to stop the GUI restarting itself after an upgrade.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colours in the command line output.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME, XDG_DATA_HOME, XDG_DATA_DIRS
Where the per\-user files and the application launchers are looked for.
.SH UNINSTALLING
.SS Packages
Use the package manager; each package stops the service and runs \fBvpnman cleanup\fR for you, so no kill\-switch rule
is left behind:
.PP
.nf
sudo apt remove vpnman          # keep /etc/vpnman        (sudo apt purge vpnman  deletes it too)
sudo dnf remove vpnman          # keeps /etc/vpnman
sudo pacman \-R vpnman
sudo xbps\-remove vpnman         # Void
sudo apk del vpnman             # Alpine
.fi
.PP
Profiles and settings in \fI/etc/vpnman\fR are kept unless the package manager purges configuration (Debian
\fBpurge\fR); delete the directory yourself for a clean slate.
.SS Installed with install.sh
Run the installer from the extracted source directory again with \fB\-\-uninstall\fR (use the same \fB\-\-prefix\fR you
installed with):
.PP
.nf
sudo ./install.sh \-\-uninstall            # keeps /etc/vpnman (profiles, settings)
sudo ./install.sh \-\-uninstall \-\-purge    # also deletes /etc/vpnman
.fi
.PP
It stops and disables the service for your init system, runs \fBvpnman cleanup \-\-force\fR (kill switch, app bypass
rules, routing rule and cgroups), removes the programs, the Python package, launcher, icons, metadata, man page,
completions and the service definition, refreshes the icon caches, and finally makes sure the \fIinet vpnman\fR table is
gone so a stale kill switch can never outlive the software.
.SS Leftovers it does not touch
.IP \(bu 2
\fI/etc/vpnman\fR without \fB\-\-purge\fR; the \fBvpnman\fR group (\fBsudo groupdel vpnman\fR);
.IP \(bu 2
per\-user files: \fI~/.config/vpnman/\fR and \fI~/.config/autostart/io.github.smiley_mcsmiles.VPNMan.desktop\fR
(or run \fBvpnman autostart \-\-login\-app off\fR before uninstalling);
.IP \(bu 2
the VPN programs, stunnel and other dependencies, which VPNMan only uses.
.PP
Back up first if you may want your profiles again: \fBvpnman backup export FILE\fR.
.SS Removing by hand
Stop and disable the service (\fBsudo vpnman service disable\fR; then \fBsudo vpnman service uninstall\fR), run
\fBsudo vpnman cleanup \-\-force\fR, and delete the files listed under INSTALLATION and FILES.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (for \fBstatus\fR: connected).
.TP
.B 1
An error, a failed connection, or a failed \fBleaktest\fR check.
.TP
.B 2
The background service is not running.
.TP
.B 3
\fBstatus\fR: not connected.
.TP
.B 10
\fBupdate\fR: a newer release exists.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted (Ctrl+C).
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.TP
.B Run vpnman doctor
It checks the daemon, firewall, init system, launcher, icons, tray and protocols, and says what is missing.
.TP
.B "error: ... daemon ..."  (exit 2)
\fBsudo vpnman service enable\fR; look at \fBvpnman logs\fR or the init system's log (see FILES).
.TP
.B Offline after a crash or with the kill switch stuck
\fBsudo vpnman cleanup \-\-force\fR (or \fBvpnman lock off\fR if the daemon runs).
.TP
.B Menu entry or icon missing
\fBsudo vpnman doctor \-\-fix\fR, then log out and in. Installing with \fB\-\-prefix /usr\fR avoids \fBXDG_DATA_DIRS\fR problems.
.TP
.B "Unrecognized option" from OpenVPN
VPNMan already removes Windows\-only options. If OpenVPN refuses another option, the log shows the exact command
line and the offending line of the runtime copy; remove that option from the profile (Windows clients often export
options Linux does not know).
.TP
.B Login rejected
The password or username is wrong: run \fBvpnman connect\fR again and enter them, or \fBvpnman edit PROFILE username=... password=...\fR.
.TP
.B An application cannot reach the network under the kill switch
Add it with \fBvpnman bypass add\fR, or its destination with \fBvpnman routes add\fR, or allow it in
\fBnetlock.whitelist_out\fR.
.TP
.B The proxy does not start
\fBvpnman proxy status\fR shows the reason. Is \fBxray\fR installed (\fBvpnman doctor\fR)? Does the log (\fBvpnman logs\fR)
show lines from Xray (\fBproxy\fR level)? In \fBvpn_proxy\fR order it waits for the VPN. A share link that Xray rejects
usually has a transport or Reality parameter missing: import the link again from its source.
.TP
.B Proxy then VPN does not connect
Check that the profile is OpenVPN or WireGuard with a single server, that the proxy server carries UDP if the profile is
UDP, and that the proxy server address resolves. The kill switch is only open to the proxy server in this order.
.TP
.B Bypass or include mode does nothing
It needs nft, ip and cgroup v2 (check \fBvpnman bypass list\fR for the reason). WireGuard with IPv6 disabled by sysctl
needs IPv6 enabled again (\fBnet.ipv6.conf.all.disable_ipv6=0\fR) because \fBwg\-quick\fR configures it.
.TP
.B "resolvconf: signature mismatch" on disconnect
Harmless; fixed in current versions by turning OpenVPN's own DNS handling off.
.SH SECURITY
The daemon runs as root and is the only component with privileges. Profiles, settings and backups can contain
passwords and private keys, so \fI/etc/vpnman\fR is mode 700 and backups are written mode 600 but unencrypted. The
socket checks the identity of every client. Credentials are handed to VPN programs through private files in \fI/run/vpnman/conn\fR (for example OpenVPN's
\fB\-\-auth\-user\-pass\fR file), not on the command line. Config files imported from others can contain scripts (\fBup\fR, \fBdown\fR with
\fBscript\-security 2\fR): only import profiles you trust. \fBvpnman update\fR and the GUI's update check contact
github.com as the user and are never started automatically unless you enable the daily check.
